# Enamel Vector Electro

**Ingredients:** CARNATIC MORSING + DETROIT ELECTRO + MRIDANGAM + PREPARED TACK PIANO + BASS FLUTE + DRY ANALOG SUB

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a late-night calibration session inside a decommissioned dental-materials research laboratory in Chennai. Morsing player Meena Varadarajan was recording jaw-harp strokes through a throat microphone to study how mouth shape changed the overtone spectrum. In the next room, Detroit-born producer Aaron Bell was testing stripped electro patterns on an analog drum machine. The breakthrough came when they stopped treating the morsing as percussion and began treating the player’s mouth cavity as a manually swept resonant filter. Short electro bass phrases were then written to answer specific vowel-shaped morsing resonances, while mridangam, prepared tack piano, and bass flute supplied fixed acoustic reference points.

Enamel Vector Electro is a dry, high-definition electro form built around the fact that a morsing produces one fundamental mechanical pulse but many different audible colors depending on the performer’s tongue, jaw, breath, and oral cavity. The instrument becomes a human resonant synthesizer without electronics replacing its physical source.

The morsing is close-miked at the mouth and simultaneously captured by a small throat/contact microphone. Players use inward and outward plucks, tongue closures, glottal pulses, silent breath shaping, jaw shifts, and rapid vowel-like cavity changes. Instead of long folk-style rhythmic runs, phrases are reduced to short cells whose overtone contour can be repeated precisely enough to function like filter automation.

Detroit electro supplies the machine grid: syncopated 808-style kicks, short snares, rimshots, metallic hats, tom pulses, handclaps, and angular mono-synth bass. The groove remains robotic in placement but sparse enough to expose the human micro-timing and formant motion of the morsing.

The central compositional device is the **Palatal Vector**. Each four-bar section defines a mouth-shape trajectory for the morsing—closed to open, low cavity to high cavity, narrow vowel to broad vowel—while the electronic bass moves in the opposite register direction. If the morsing resonance rises, the bass may descend; if the jaw cavity darkens, the synth line may brighten. Arrangement becomes a counterpoint between physical formant motion and sequenced pitch.

Mridangam is used as an acoustic timing authority rather than as ornament. Dry strokes such as tha, dhin, nam, and chapu-derived accents are arranged in compact response cells around the electro drum machine. Selected machine hits are removed so the drum skin owns that transient completely.

Prepared tack piano provides hard fixed-pitch coordinates. Thumbtacks, felt, paper, and selective muting create brittle upper-register dyads, deadened octaves, and short metallic chord fragments. These attacks confirm the exact harmonic center after the morsing has created the illusion of pitch movement through formant change.

Bass flute supplies breath and low-mid continuity. It uses dry key noise, short overblown attacks, hollow pedal tones, and descending chromatic fragments. It never becomes a lyrical solo instrument; its role is to occupy the air left after the morsing pulse stops.

Analog sub-bass remains mono and disciplined. Short sine or triangle notes reinforce only selected electro-bass fundamentals and disappear before the next morsing phrase. Continuous sub drones are avoided.

The defining tension is machine pitch versus mouth-shaped spectrum. The sequencer changes notes; the morsing often does not, yet the ear perceives both as moving melodic systems.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 124–134 BPM, usually 4/4. Detroit-electro syncopation with clipped 808 kicks, dry snares, metallic hats, sparse toms, machine claps, mridangam response strokes, and deliberate one-eighth- or one-quarter-note vacancies.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian, Phrygian, minor pentatonic, open fifths, minor 7ths, suspended 2nds, flattened 2nds, octave pedals, and chromatic bass pickups. Harmonic progression is limited; perceived color movement comes heavily from changing morsing formants.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Palatal Vector** — the morsing performer follows a defined mouth-cavity trajectory while the synth bass moves in complementary or contrary register.
2. **Dual-Mouth Capture** — mouth microphone and throat/contact signal are recorded separately so overtone color and mechanical pulse can be balanced without replacing the acoustic source.
3. **Skin-for-Machine Exchange** — selected drum-machine hits are removed and replaced by isolated mridangam strokes rather than layered beneath them.
4. **Tack Coordinate** — prepared-piano dyads land after complex formant sweeps to re-establish exact pitch reference.
5. **Breath Residue** — bass flute enters only after important morsing cells, extending the physical air gesture after the metal tongue has stopped.
6. **No-Vocoder Rule** — spectral motion must come primarily from the performer’s mouth and jaw; vocoders, formant plugins, and automated vowel filters are excluded from the lead sound.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through faster morsing pluck density, more extreme mouth-shape changes, tighter electro syncopation, brighter tack-piano attacks, denser mridangam exchanges, and more active bass-flute key noise. Peaks remain dry and sharply separated.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Enamel Vector Electro formed inside a retired dental-materials and prosthetics research laboratory in Chennai. The building retained ceramic sample drawers, plaster jaw molds, stainless worktables, articulated dental models, vacuum-forming machines, enamel shade cards, analog hardness gauges, fluorescent inspection lamps, and small acoustic booths once used for equipment-noise testing.

Morsing specialist **Meena Varadarajan** had been invited to record overtone studies for a university acoustics archive. She discovered that a throat microphone revealed a second rhythmic layer: tiny internal pressure changes and jaw movements that were nearly invisible on a standard room microphone.

Detroit producer **Aaron Bell** was using an adjacent test room during an electronic-music exchange program. His first attempt placed Meena over a complete electro beat and failed because the morsing became another high-frequency percussion texture. He removed most hats and asked her to repeat a single pluck while slowly changing only her mouth shape. The resulting spectral sweep sounded like a filter moving across a synth even though the metal tongue was not changing pitch.

Mridangam player **Karthik Raman** joined to translate selected machine accents back into skin. Pianist **Latha Iyer** prepared an old upright test-room piano with tacks and felt so it could provide exact brittle pitch markers. Bass-flutist **Nolan Price** completed the group after discovering that low air tones could extend the decay of a morsing gesture without obscuring its overtone field.

Their first performances were called **bite maps**. A simple diagram of mouth shapes was projected above the band, but it controlled nothing electronically. Meena followed the diagram physically while Aaron wrote bass patterns in opposite motion. Audiences could hear a filter sweep and then see that it had been produced entirely by jaw position.

The scene developed a visual language of ivory ceramic, pale mint tile, stainless steel, graphite measurement marks, enamel shade cards, matte black instruments, amber meter lamps, and dark indigo clothing. The unofficial rule is that any spectral motion which can be performed by the mouth must not be automated.

