# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Enamel Shade C Moves Without Changing Pitch

**Story & Context:** The debut opens at 128 BPM with one dry morsing pluck repeated every half bar. Meena keeps the metal tongue at the same fundamental while moving her mouth from a dark closed cavity toward a bright open vowel. Aaron answers with a descending three-note electro-bass pattern, creating contrary motion between spectral brightness and actual pitch.

Karthik replaces every fourth machine snare with one dry mridangam stroke. Latha enters after the first complete formant sweep with a brittle prepared-piano fifth, proving that the tonal center has not moved. Nolan adds a hollow bass-flute note only after Meena closes her mouth shape again.

At the midpoint, Aaron removes the bass entirely while Meena repeats the same palatal vector faster. The perceived melodic motion survives without pitched accompaniment. The final cycle ends on a neutral mouth position and one unprepared piano note.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Macro image of an enamel shade card marked C beside a brass morsing, a mouth-shape diagram changing from narrow to open across graph paper, analog bass sequencer lights in shallow focus. No full lyrics; spoken fragments limited to “shade C,” “same tongue,” “move the mouth,” and “pitch stays.”

## Track 2: Throat Mic Finds the Missing Snare

**Story & Context:** Aaron programs a 132 BPM electro pattern with one snare intentionally absent every second bar. Meena plays a muted morsing stroke into the throat microphone exactly at that vacancy, creating a low mechanical pulse almost without the bright mouth-mic overtone.

Karthik gradually surrounds that pulse with mridangam answers, while Latha uses deadened tack-piano octaves to mark the four-bar boundary. Nolan performs short breath-only bass-flute attacks with no stable pitch, extending the percussive air character.

Halfway through, the mouth microphone is muted for eight bars, leaving only throat vibration, skin drum, machine kick, and piano mechanics. When the bright morsing channel returns, the same groove suddenly appears wider without any added reverb.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close side-profile of a throat contact microphone beneath the jaw, empty snare position circled on a drum-machine pattern sheet, morsing silhouette reflected in stainless steel. Spoken fragments limited to “missing snare,” “throat only,” “leave the top out,” and “bring the mouth back.”

## Track 3: Bite Map Ends at the Open Fifth

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 124 BPM and uses the slowest palatal movement on the EP. Meena follows a printed sequence of four mouth positions while Aaron holds an open-fifth synth pedal for sixteen bars. Instead of changing harmony, Latha shifts the preparation of one piano string between sections, altering attack color while preserving the same interval.

Karthik begins with sparse mridangam strokes and gradually removes them, reversing the usual build. Nolan introduces a descending bass-flute chromatic fragment each time Meena reaches the widest mouth position. The fragment becomes shorter on every repetition until only one breathy key click remains.

In the final passage, all electronics stop except a faint analog clock pulse. Meena plays one last morsing stroke, opens the mouth cavity fully, then closes it without another pluck. The spectral change continues briefly from the decaying tongue vibration. The last sound is the prepared piano’s damper returning to the string.

